WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.826
Arrays are among the oldest and most

00:00:02.897 --> 00:00:04.721
important data structures.

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:07.809
Almost every language has them, and stacks

00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:10.476
queues, heaps and hash tables are all

00:00:10.546 --> 00:00:12.020
built on top of them.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.388
Think of a row of numbered pigeonholes.

00:00:15.388 --> 00:00:18.869
Every hole has a number, starting from zero.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:20.726
If you know the number

00:00:20.726 --> 00:00:22.970
you go straight to the hole.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.233
So here is the question for this part.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.763
How does the computer reach cell

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.680
number four in one step

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.900
without walking past cells zero to three?

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.120
And why does the numbering start at zero

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.040
not at one?

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.325
An abstract data type, or ADT

00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:44.570
says what operations a type offers

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.275
not how they are implemented.

00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:49.529
The array ADT has three.

00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:53.586
createArray(n) makes an array of n cells

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:55.660
in Java, new int[n].

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.331
retrieve(arr, i) takes the array and an index i

00:00:59.331 --> 00:01:02.901
and returns the item stored in position i.

00:01:02.901 --> 00:01:05.309
In Java we write arr[i].

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:08.547
Retrieve position three of this array

00:01:08.547 --> 00:01:10.290
and you get fifteen.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.686
And store(arr, i, item)

00:01:13.686 --> 00:01:16.636
puts the item into position i

00:01:16.636 --> 00:01:18.920
arr[i] = item.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.350
The ADT hides how the array is laid out

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.431
so we can design algorithms against it.

00:01:25.431 --> 00:01:27.587
Linear search, for example

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:31.053
only needs retrieve: look at positions zero

00:01:31.053 --> 00:01:32.670
one, two, and so on.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.332
Here is an algorithm written

00:01:35.406 --> 00:01:38.678
against the ADT: the element-wise product of

00:01:38.752 --> 00:01:40.388
two vectors a1 and a2

00:01:40.388 --> 00:01:42.916
also called the Hadamard product.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.774
Our method takes the two

00:01:44.849 --> 00:01:46.410
arrays as parameters.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.745
createArray makes a3

00:01:48.745 --> 00:01:51.091
with as many cells as a1.

00:01:51.091 --> 00:01:54.880
A new int array starts filled with zeros.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.240
Then, for every i

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.707
retrieve a1[i] and a2[i], multiply

00:02:01.707 --> 00:02:05.503
and store the product in a3[i].

00:02:05.503 --> 00:02:08.183
One times four is four.

00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:10.752
Two times five is ten.

00:02:10.752 --> 00:02:13.990
Three times six is eighteen.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.407
The method returns a3

00:02:16.407 --> 00:02:20.540
and the program prints 4, 10, 18.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.020
The method first checks that the two

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.355
arrays have the same length.

00:02:26.436 --> 00:02:29.174
The demo also calls it with a1 and

00:02:29.255 --> 00:02:31.913
a new array of only two elements

00:02:31.913 --> 00:02:32.960
1 and 2.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:34.556
Without the check

00:02:34.556 --> 00:02:36.911
the loop would ask for a2[2]

00:02:36.911 --> 00:02:38.850
a cell that does not exist.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.689
With it, the method throws an exception

00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:43.836
and the program prints: Error

00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:46.190
arrays must have the same length.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.590
Create, retrieve, store

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.812
and care with the length

00:02:50.812 --> 00:02:53.460
that is all this method needs.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.662
Now the Java syntax.

00:02:55.662 --> 00:02:58.582
Give the length of an array a name

00:02:58.582 --> 00:03:01.988
here a constant, N_JUDGES, equal to 5

00:03:01.988 --> 00:03:04.664
so the number appears only once.

00:03:04.664 --> 00:03:07.989
new double[N_JUDGES] makes 5 cells

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:08.800
all 0.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.704
The loop stores a score in every cell

00:03:12.704 --> 00:03:15.228
7 + 0.5 * i.

00:03:15.228 --> 00:03:17.566
So cell 0 gets 7

00:03:17.566 --> 00:03:20.372
then 7.5, 8

00:03:20.372 --> 00:03:22.990
8.5, and 9.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.216
Notice the loop condition

00:03:25.216 --> 00:03:27.452
i < scores.length.

00:03:27.452 --> 00:03:29.905
Length is a field of every array

00:03:29.905 --> 00:03:31.998
written without parentheses

00:03:31.998 --> 00:03:35.100
and it is fixed when the array is created.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.615
The index can be any int expression.

00:03:38.615 --> 00:03:41.957
With a equal to 1 and b equal to 4

00:03:42.039 --> 00:03:43.913
scores of (a + b) over

00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:45.707
2 is scores[2]

00:03:45.788 --> 00:03:48.315
because dividing one integer by

00:03:48.397 --> 00:03:50.680
another rounds towards 0

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.473
5 / 2 is 2.

00:03:52.473 --> 00:03:55.000
So it prints 8.0.

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.231
To add up the scores we do

00:03:57.304 --> 00:04:00.198
not need the positions, only the values.

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:01.573
The for-each loop

00:04:01.573 --> 00:04:03.599
for (double value : scores)

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:05.842
hands us each element in turn

00:04:05.842 --> 00:04:08.230
from the first cell to the last.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.286
The total is 40.

00:04:10.286 --> 00:04:13.697
Use the indexed loop when you need i

00:04:13.697 --> 00:04:16.300
or want to change the cells

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:20.250
use for-each when you only read the values.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.428
So how is an array stored?

00:04:23.428 --> 00:04:25.875
Take this array of 12 ints.

00:04:25.951 --> 00:04:28.016
An array is a collection of

00:04:28.093 --> 00:04:29.851
items of the same type

00:04:29.851 --> 00:04:32.069
stored in contiguous memory

00:04:32.069 --> 00:04:35.280
the cells sit side by side, with no gaps

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.880
and every cell has the same size.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.427
The address of the first cell

00:04:40.503 --> 00:04:43.760
is the base address; say it is 200.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.654
An int takes 4 bytes

00:04:45.654 --> 00:04:48.684
so cell 1 starts at 204

00:04:48.684 --> 00:04:51.032
cell 2 at 208

00:04:51.032 --> 00:04:52.926
then 212

00:04:52.926 --> 00:04:55.350
216, and so on.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.005
So the address of cell i is the base

00:04:59.005 --> 00:05:02.400
plus i times the size of one element.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.420
This one line is the whole secret of arrays.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.213
Where is cell 11?

00:05:09.213 --> 00:05:13.111
200, plus 11 times 4

00:05:13.111 --> 00:05:15.640
244.

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.870
The computer does one multiplication

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.910
and one addition, whatever the index

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.390
and however long the array is.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.550
So reading or writing a[i]

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.190
takes constant time, O(1).

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.590
This is called random access

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:35.190
and it is the defining strength of an array.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.942
It also explains why indices start at 0.

00:05:39.021 --> 00:05:40.840
The index is the offset

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.660
from the base address

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.220
cell 0 is 0 cells away from the start.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.863
Java never shows real addresses

00:05:48.863 --> 00:05:51.157
so our base address is made up

00:05:51.228 --> 00:05:53.307
In C you could print them and

00:05:53.378 --> 00:05:55.170
see exactly this pattern.

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:56.704
Your turn.

00:05:56.704 --> 00:05:59.663
Here is an array d of doubles

00:05:59.663 --> 00:06:02.622
each double takes 8 bytes.

00:06:02.622 --> 00:06:06.630
The array starts at address 1000.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:08.437
Where is d[5]?

00:06:08.513 --> 00:06:10.660
And where would d[5] be

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:12.730
if indices started at 1?

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.260
Pause the video and work it out.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.953
d[5] is 5 cells from the start

00:06:23.953 --> 00:06:27.749
1000 plus 5 times 8

00:06:27.749 --> 00:06:30.280
1040.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.363
If the numbering started at 1

00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:35.841
the cell numbered 5 would

00:06:35.926 --> 00:06:37.463
be the fifth cell

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.770
4 cells from the start

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:42.674
1000 plus (5 minus 1)

00:06:42.674 --> 00:06:43.784
times 8

00:06:43.784 --> 00:06:47.030
which is 1032.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.378
So every access would need

00:06:49.455 --> 00:06:51.068
an extra subtraction.

00:06:51.145 --> 00:06:53.142
Starting at zero makes the

00:06:53.219 --> 00:06:55.140
index exactly the offset.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:58.265
Java has two kinds of arrays.

00:06:58.265 --> 00:07:01.862
Here is an int array, nums, holding 10

00:07:01.862 --> 00:07:03.880
20, 30, 40.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.214
In a primitive array, of int, double

00:07:07.214 --> 00:07:08.706
char or boolean

00:07:08.706 --> 00:07:12.040
the cells hold the values themselves.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:13.574
Here is names

00:07:13.574 --> 00:07:16.619
a new String array of length 3.

00:07:16.619 --> 00:07:18.310
In an object array

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.594
the cells hold references

00:07:20.679 --> 00:07:22.962
arrows to objects that live

00:07:23.047 --> 00:07:25.161
somewhere else in memory.

00:07:25.161 --> 00:07:28.206
A new one holds null in every cell

00:07:28.206 --> 00:07:29.390
no arrow yet.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:33.075
names[0] = "Ada" puts an arrow

00:07:33.163 --> 00:07:35.884
to the string Ada in cell zero.

00:07:35.972 --> 00:07:38.517
names[1] = "Alan" does

00:07:38.605 --> 00:07:40.448
the same in cell one.

00:07:40.448 --> 00:07:43.871
Printed, the array is Ada, Alan, null

00:07:43.871 --> 00:07:45.100
length 3.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.170
In both cases the variable itself

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:49.370
nums or names

00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.930
holds a reference to the array

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:53.770
an array is an object.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.770
A Python list works like

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.450
a Java object array

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.610
every cell is a reference.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:04.492
The general form is type[] name

00:08:04.492 --> 00:08:07.324
equals new type[length].

00:08:07.429 --> 00:08:09.947
Java fills the new array

00:08:10.052 --> 00:08:12.150
with default values.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.843
0 for whole numbers

00:08:14.843 --> 00:08:17.797
0.0 for doubles

00:08:17.797 --> 00:08:22.380
false for booleans, and null for references.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.828
A char array gets the null character

00:08:25.828 --> 00:08:26.749
code 0.

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:29.930
A new Java array never holds garbage.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.448
Using an index outside 0 to length

00:08:33.528 --> 00:08:35.910
minus 1 is a run-time error.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:38.292
This array has 5 elements

00:08:38.372 --> 00:08:40.516
so the last one is at index

00:08:40.595 --> 00:08:43.137
length minus 1, which is 4

00:08:43.137 --> 00:08:46.710
and the program prints: last element, 50.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:49.084
The next line asks for the

00:08:49.162 --> 00:08:51.732
element at index length, that is

00:08:51.732 --> 00:08:54.067
index 5: one past the end.

00:08:54.067 --> 00:08:56.092
Java checks every access

00:08:56.169 --> 00:08:58.817
so the program stops with an array

00:08:58.894 --> 00:09:01.230
index out of bounds exception

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:04.500
index 5 out of bounds for length 5.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:06.088
C does not check.

00:09:06.161 --> 00:09:08.637
It silently reads whatever happens

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.312
to be in memory there

00:09:10.312 --> 00:09:12.570
which is much harder to debug.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:16.950
The classic mistake is being off by one.

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:20.845
The last element is at length - 1

00:09:20.845 --> 00:09:22.270
not at length.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:26.718
So a loop over the array runs while i is

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.918
less than the length of the array: 0

00:09:30.918 --> 00:09:35.220
1, 2, 3, 4, and then it stops.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.499
Write <= 

00:09:37.499 --> 00:09:40.893
and the loop tries index 5, and crashes.

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:44.354
Why do we need arrays at all?

00:09:44.354 --> 00:09:47.111
Suppose a class has five students

00:09:47.111 --> 00:09:50.497
and we keep their marks in five variables

00:09:50.497 --> 00:09:51.836
v1 to v5.

00:09:51.836 --> 00:09:55.302
To add them up we must name every variable.

00:09:55.381 --> 00:09:58.137
With 500 students we would

00:09:58.216 --> 00:10:00.106
need 500 names

00:10:00.106 --> 00:10:02.390
and a 500-term sum.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.127
An array represents many values

00:10:05.204 --> 00:10:06.590
with one variable

00:10:06.667 --> 00:10:08.823
marks[0] up to

00:10:08.900 --> 00:10:10.825
marks[499].

00:10:10.825 --> 00:10:13.212
And one loop handles them all

00:10:13.212 --> 00:10:15.060
however many there are.

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:18.873
A loop visits marks[0], marks[1], marks[2]

00:10:18.873 --> 00:10:20.331
marks[3] and marks[4]

00:10:20.331 --> 00:10:24.250
and it would do the same for 500.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:25.731
To sum up.

00:10:25.731 --> 00:10:29.786
One: an array is a row of cells of one type

00:10:29.786 --> 00:10:33.750
side by side in memory, numbered from zero.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.491
Two: the address of a[i] is the base

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:40.257
plus i times the element size

00:10:40.257 --> 00:10:44.005
so access by index is one step, O(1).

00:10:44.005 --> 00:10:47.306
Three: primitive arrays hold values

00:10:47.306 --> 00:10:50.072
object arrays hold references

00:10:50.072 --> 00:10:52.660
and Java checks every index.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:53.670
Try this.

00:10:53.744 --> 00:10:55.505
Our array of ints starts

00:10:55.578 --> 00:10:57.266
at address 200.

00:10:57.266 --> 00:11:00.347
Where are arr[6] and arr[9]?

00:11:00.421 --> 00:11:03.356
Then check your answers with the address

00:11:03.429 --> 00:11:05.410
table program in the notes.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:09.184
In part two, we meet the kinds of arrays

00:11:09.184 --> 00:11:10.770
fixed and dynamic

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:14.110
two- and three-dimensional, and jagged

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:17.200
and the loops that visit every cell.

